{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target answers the cross-city variation question by claiming the single best modulator is urban vegetation/irrigation: drier cities with less green space have a LARGER nocturnal UHI. This is empirically wrong for the most important arid-city class. In arid climates, the rural surroundings are also moisture-limited, so the rural-urban contrast in evapotranspiration is small or even reversed (the irrigated city becomes an oasis), yet these cities often show among the largest nighttime UHI intensities. The target even lists as a countercondition 'If urban areas in arid climates... fail to show a pronounced nocturnal UHI' — but arid cities like Phoenix do show pronounced nocturnal UHI despite minimal rural ET, which under the target's own causal story (the gap scales with the rural-urban ET contrast) should not happen. The well-established dominant cross-city modulator for nighttime UHI is geometry/sky-view factor and urban thermal mass (reduced radiative cooling and stored-heat release), not vegetation/humidity. By choosing latent-heat contrast as the single mechanism, the target contradicts the arid-city evidence and fails the problem's cross-city requirement.","counterexample":[{"city":"Phoenix, AZ (arid)","observation":"Pronounced nocturnal UHI persists despite low rural evapotranspiration; irrigated urban areas can be cooler than surrounding desert during day, yet nighttime UHI remains large — inconsistent with a vegetation/humidity-gap mechanism."},{"city":"High-density compact cities (e.g., dense Asian/European cores)","observation":"Largest nocturnal UHI occurs where street canyons and low sky-view factor suppress longwave radiative cooling, even when urban vegetation differences alone cannot explain the magnitude."}],"premise":"The target asserts that drier cities with less vegetation have a larger nocturnal UHI because the rural-urban contrast in latent heat loss is maximized, and lists arid-city failure as a falsifying condition.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"0123456789abcdef01234567","quote":"The single best mechanism for cross-city variation is the availability of urban vegetation and irrigation: cities with less green space and lower humidity exhibit a more pronounced nocturnal UHI because the rural-urban divergence in latent heat depletion is larger."},{"block":"000000000000target001","quote":"If urban areas in arid climates, where rural evapotranspiration is also low, fail to show a pronounced nocturnal UHI."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}